WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
We have watched poisons block the proton flow, or let it leak around ATP synthase.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.300
Now look inside. Protons fall through, and ATP comes out.

3
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.280
How does a falling proton pay for a chemical bond?

4
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:24.133
In the membrane, a ring of c subunits, the rotor, presses against a fixed subunit.

5
00:00:25.373 --> 00:00:29.253
A proton enters a half channel and lands on an acid on the ring.

6
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:33.673
Only a loaded subunit can turn into the oily membrane.

7
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:39.967
The gradient biases the ring's random jostling, so it steps mostly one way.

8
00:00:41.287 --> 00:00:44.667
Nearly a full turn later, the proton leaves into the matrix.

9
00:00:45.947 --> 00:00:48.127
The ring is bolted to a bent shaft, gamma.

10
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.920
The shaft reaches into the head, in the matrix.

11
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.140
Three alpha and three beta subunits sit around it like orange segments.

12
00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.760
The bent shaft presses differently on each beta, holding each in its own shape.

13
00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:08.263
Each beta subunit is where ATP is made, from ADP and phosphate.

14
00:01:09.683 --> 00:01:11.523
So what does the proton's energy buy?

15
00:01:12.783 --> 00:01:14.463
Making the bond, or something else?

16
00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:22.887
Paul Boyer turned the question inside out.

17
00:01:24.147 --> 00:01:27.767
As the shaft turns, each beta site cycles through three shapes.

18
00:01:29.107 --> 00:01:30.827
Open takes in ADP and phosphate.

19
00:01:32.427 --> 00:01:38.147
Loose holds them. In Tight, ATP forms on its own, held too firmly to leave.

20
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.540
On the enzyme, making the bond costs almost nothing.

21
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.740
Here is the idea. Protons turn the shaft, and the turning pays above all to pry

22
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.000
finished ATP loose.

23
00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.240
It also drives the binding of ADP and phosphate.

24
00:01:56.383 --> 00:02:02.223
Each one hundred twenty degree turn moves every site one step and releases one ATP.

25
00:02:03.763 --> 00:02:06.423
John Walker later caught all three in one crystal.

26
00:02:07.723 --> 00:02:12.203
One site held an ATP look-alike, one ADP, and one was empty.

27
00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:14.623
Now count the price.

28
00:02:15.963 --> 00:02:19.403
The vertebrate ring has eight c subunits, the smallest known.

29
00:02:20.703 --> 00:02:24.343
Eight protons make one full turn, which releases three ATP.

30
00:02:25.743 --> 00:02:28.463
That is about two point seven protons per ATP.

31
00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:34.147
A translocase then swaps each ATP out of the matrix for ADP.

32
00:02:35.507 --> 00:02:38.367
That swap moves charge, so the voltage takes a toll.

33
00:02:39.847 --> 00:02:41.587
Phosphate comes in with a proton too.

34
00:02:43.187 --> 00:02:46.447
Together, about three point seven protons per exported ATP.

35
00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.087
Follow one proton.

36
00:02:50.307 --> 00:02:54.147
It rides the ring nearly once around, and pays for an eighth of a turn.

37
00:02:55.387 --> 00:02:57.047
The bond costs almost nothing.

38
00:02:58.307 --> 00:03:02.807
What the proton buys, above all, is the moment a finished ATP lets go.
